+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers edit the same visit independently — two workers can correct one visit after comparing notes, and a tablet can be offline for 48 hours, so edits arrive days after the state they were based on was fetched. The record store must define what a visit's current record is when these copies arrive, without treating arrival order as evidence of authority.
+
+Both approaches below preserve every original submission; that is an inherited invariant, not a criterion that separates them. What the choice actually settles is the shape of the submission envelope, the volume and nature of coordinator review, which record states can enter the weekly export, and whether the server carries rules for combining field edits.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Weighted criteria** (importance in parentheses):
+
+- **Preservation of whole-visit scientific intent** (major) — whether the mechanism can combine edits that are scientifically related without noticing. Turns on the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and the non-goal of inferring scientific intent.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** (major) — the code and, more durably, the rules that must be kept correct as the visit schema evolves.
+- **Review workload and export readiness** (moderate) — how much coordinator work each arriving edit creates and how many visits are ready each Friday.
+- **Operability by two coordinators** (moderate) — turn on the tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+- **Submission and tablet storage size** (minor) — whether tablets carry base snapshots or only base revision identifiers.
+
+**Latent criteria probed and set aside:** time-to-first-release and automatic resolution of trivial edits were considered. Time-to-release favors the simpler option but is not what the choice is about; automatic resolution is a benefit both options partially share (whole-visit leaves a single sequential head settled without review).
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each edit names the revision it was edited from and submits a complete visit. Edits from the same starting revision become competing complete revisions; a coordinator resolves the competition by selecting one survivor. The server never combines payloads. A visit with a single head — the ordinary sequential-edit case — is settled without review; only genuine competition reaches a coordinator.
+
+**Demonstration.** A worker submits `r1 (base r0)`, another submits `r2 (base r0)`. Both are heads, the visit is unresolved, and both revisions appear for review. A coordinator selects one; the other is retained as history.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- Preservation of whole-visit scientific intent: **strong** — no field edit is ever recombined, so relationships the server cannot model are never crossed.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **strong** — the store derives current state from base links and resolutions; there are no combination rules to maintain as fields change.
+- Review workload and export readiness: **weak** — every competing edit, including unrelated ones such as an observer-name correction beside a new species, enters review, and its visit stays out of the export until resolved.
+- Operability: **partial** — the coordinator workflow is a comparison-and-select screen rather than rule maintenance, but the queue is larger.
+- Submission and tablet storage size: **strong** — a submission is one complete visit and a base revision identifier; tablets need keep only revision IDs, not base snapshots.
+
+**Summary:** Preserves each submitted visit as a unit at the cost of a larger review queue.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each edit supplies its base state and a structured set of changes. Disjoint field changes — a corrected observer name beside an added species — combine automatically; overlapping changes to the same field require review. The server needs maintained rules for each field kind: scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries.
+
+**Demonstration.** From base `r0`, one edit changes `observers` and another adds a `species_cover` entry; the server combines them into one current record with no review. Two edits that both change the same species' cover overlap and go to review.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- Preservation of whole-visit scientific intent: **weak** — automatically combined fields can cross scientific relationships the rules do not represent; a rule that treats a species identification and its cover estimate as independent can produce a record neither worker submitted.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **weak** — combination rules must be authored and maintained for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and re-examined whenever the visit schema changes.
+- Review workload and export readiness: **strong** — unrelated edits combine without review and more visits are ready by Friday.
+- Operability: **partial** — the coordinator interface is smaller, but rule maintenance is ongoing work that can drift from scientific intent.
+- Submission and tablet storage size: **weak** — tablets must retain and transmit base snapshots so the server can compute deltas.
+
+**Summary:** Cuts review volume and readies more visits, at the cost of maintained merge rules and the risk of unnoticed scientific recombination.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions with coordinator resolution.** The decision turns on the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*: a record is always exactly a visit a worker submitted, which the field-aware option cannot guarantee once rules combine fields. Review workload (moderate) and export readiness (moderate) cut the other way and are accepted as the cost. A larger review queue is a burden two coordinators can see and drain; a silently recombined scientific record is not, which is the failure the project exists to prevent.
+
+The choice obligates downstream work:
+
+- **Tablet-sync** must retain base revision identifiers with each local draft and submit the edit's base alongside the complete visit. It must surface pending competing revisions so a worker whose edit is awaiting resolution understands why the visit is not yet current.
+- **Review-export** must provide a revision comparison screen for competing revisions and exclude unresolved visits from the CSV. A visit re-enters the export set once a coordinator resolves it.
+
+The field-aware approach is not ruled out by the project's requirements — a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval would leave it viable. It is set aside so the store does not carry merge rules that can combine scientifically related edits without noticing. Reconsider it if the review queue outgrows the coordinators' capacity; that would reopen the merge-rule burden and the silent-recombination risk together, and would change both downstream contracts above.
